from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "9. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "Claim: the case against annihilationism is not strengthened by weakening the other side. The writers who hold that the lost cease to exist argue from Scripture, and their strongest arguments are stated here in their own best form, beside the writers who answer them. Each objection is answered from Scripture, and what is granted is said first."),
("sub", "9.1 Who is named and how"),
("p", "On the side that holds the lost cease to exist, five writers are named. Basil Atkinson, an English conditionalist of the last century, is known here through Robert Peterson’s study of his exegesis, and his positions are described as Peterson reports them. Edward Fudge, in The Fire That Consumes, gives the fullest scriptural case for conditional immortality in English. John Stott, in a dialogue with David Edwards published in 1988, said that he held the view, tentatively, and asked for it to be debated. Clark Pinnock argued in an article of 1990 that the finally impenitent are destroyed. John Wenham, in The Goodness of God, took the same position. The primary texts of Fudge, Stott, Pinnock and Wenham were not accessible or available at the time of this writing, and the volume describes their positions in its own words, naming the author and the work, without quotation marks."),
("p", "On the side of the volume, three writers are named. Robert Peterson, in an article on Atkinson in The Churchman, answers the exegesis of Basil Atkinson passage by passage. Marlin Woodier, in an article in Evangel, defends the traditional doctrine from the same Scriptures. Andy Saville, in an article in the Scottish Bulletin of Evangelical Theology, answers the doctrinal arguments of Stott, Pinnock, Wenham, Fudge and others. Alan Linfield’s survey of the debate in the Evangelical Review of Theology is used for the names of those who hold the conditionalist view. Each of these is quoted only where the volume has read the actual words in a free full text. The volume cites by article and does not give page numbers."),
("sub", "9.2 The objection from the words of destruction"),
("p", "The objection. The New Testament speaks of the end of the lost in the words of destruction, perishing and death, and in the Old Testament the same words describe the end of what is destroyed. When Scripture says that God is able to destroy soul and body in hell, that the end of the enemies of the cross is destruction and that the wicked shall be punished with everlasting destruction, the plain sense is that they are destroyed, and a thing that is destroyed is no more. Fudge builds the case on this, and Atkinson’s exegesis, as Peterson describes it, treats the destruction as the end of the person."),
("p", "What is granted. The words do describe an ending in many verses, as chapter 3 showed from Genesis 7, Daniel 2 and Matthew 12. The soul is not indestructible by nature, and Matthew 10:28 does not say that it is. The objection from the ordinary sense of the words is a fair one, and the volume has not tried to weaken it."),
("p", "The reply. The ordinary sense of the words is not one sense. A lost sheep is a sheep that is alive, a waste of ointment is ointment that was spent and is not gone from the world, and a man delivered to Satan “for the destruction of the flesh” is a man who is saved (1 Corinthians 5:5). Death itself is used of one who is alive. Of the son who came home the father says, “this my son was dead, and is alive again” (Luke 15:24), and the apostle says of the saints, “you hath he quickened, who were dead in trespasses and sins” (Ephesians 2:1). Woodier reads death in Genesis 2:17 as separation and not extinction, pointing to the broken fellowship and the exclusion from the garden in Genesis 3. The volume adds a plain observation from the text: the man who was told that in the day he ate he should surely die went on living, and “all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died” (Genesis 5:5). The first death cannot be read as the man’s ceasing on the day he ate. The question is therefore what the words mean in a given passage, and that is decided by the passage. Chapters 4 and 5 read the two verses the objection leans on most and found that each states a power or an end and says nothing of its manner."),
("sub", "9.3 The objection from the word eternal"),
("p", "The objection. “Eternal” describes the result of an action and not the action itself. On the reading Peterson reports of Atkinson, an adjective of this kind set on a noun of action refers to the result, so that “everlasting punishment” is a punishment done once with permanent results, as “eternal redemption” is a redemption done once. Fudge adds that the adjective has a qualitative sense as well as a quantitative one, and can describe a thing of the age to come without saying how long the experience lasts. Linfield’s survey of the debate sets the same point beside the others."),
("p", "What is granted. The adjective is used in this way in Hebrews 9:12, Hebrews 5:9 and Hebrews 6:2. Chapter 6 granted that it can describe a result and that Matthew 25:46 does not by itself decide the question."),
("p", "The reply. Peterson’s answer to Atkinson’s rule is that it is unsupported by the language and that it breaks the parallel with “life eternal”. The volume goes with the first of his points in part and with the second in full. It does not rest on a grammatical rule about nouns of action, and it does not say that the adjective carries the case. It says that the persons “go away into everlasting punishment” as the righteous go into life, and that the passages about the persons in the lake, taken with the adjective, describe persons who are in the lake and are not described as finishing. Peterson judges the conditionalist exegesis of Revelation 14:10, 11 “an evasion of the text and not a literal exegesis”, and the volume does not use words so strong. It says that an exegesis which gives the account of the smoke and passes over “no rest day nor night” has not answered the verse."),
("sub", "9.4 The objection from the fire and the worm"),
("p", "The objection. A fire that is not quenched consumes what it burns, and a worm that does not die is a worm that eats. The last verse of Isaiah is a picture of corpses, and the Lord’s words in Mark 9:48 are a quotation of it. Atkinson, in Peterson’s report, read the worm as the suffering that comes before the final destruction and the fire as the destruction. Fudge cites the unquenchable fires of Jeremiah 17:27 and Ezekiel 20:47 as fires that finished their work."),
("p", "What is granted. Isaiah 66:24 is a picture of corpses. Unquenchable fire is fire that cannot be put out, and it can end when its fuel is gone. Isaiah 34:10 uses the language of fire for ever of a land that is not burning now. Chapter 7 granted all of this."),
("p", "The reply. Peterson finds Atkinson’s exegesis of Mark 9:43, 48 “problematic on a number of counts”, and counts among them, not least, his understanding of Isaiah 66:24. The volume’s own reply is the one chapter 7 gave. The Lord does not say “carcases”. He says of a man with two hands that he is cast into hell, into the fire that never shall be quenched, where their worm dieth not. He sets the casting against entering into life, and He adds that every one shall be salted with fire (Mark 9:49). The fire is a place the living are cast into, and the picture the Lord takes from Isaiah is the picture of what is at that place. The volume marks the step as Inferred and does not claim more."),
("sub", "9.5 The objection from Revelation’s smoke"),
("p", "The objection. The smoke that goes up for ever and ever is the language of a ruin that is permanent. It is the smoke of Edom in Isaiah 34, and it is the smoke of Babylon in Revelation 19:3, and neither is a place of continuing torment. On Atkinson’s reading, as Peterson gives it, the burning sulphur consumes the wicked and the smoke rising for ever marks the eternal result of the judgment."),
("p", "What is granted. The smoke alone does not prove conscious torment, and Revelation 19:3 shows that the book uses the image of a city. The symbols of the book are real symbols."),
("p", "The reply. The volume does not stand on the smoke. It stands on the persons, who are tormented in the presence of the holy angels and of the Lamb, who have no rest day nor night, and who, in the plural statement of Revelation 20:10, include the beast and the false prophet cast in a thousand years before the devil. The objection answers the sentence about the smoke and does not answer the sentence about the persons. Peterson’s judgment of the conditionalist reading is that it must be labelled an evasion of the text. The volume’s judgment is milder and the same: the reading leaves two clauses of the third angel’s warning, and the plural statement of torment in Revelation 20:10, without an account."),
("sub", "9.6 The objection from justice"),
("p", "The objection. Endless punishment for sins done in a finite life is out of proportion, and a loving God would not inflict it. Stott, in his dialogue with Edwards, said that he found the idea hard to reconcile with the justice of God, and Wenham and Pinnock argued in the same way, as Saville reports their arguments. Pinnock and Stott also made the argument from dualism, that endless torment leaves a part of creation in rebellion for ever."),
("p", "What is granted. The objection is made in earnest and by writers who love the Scripture. A reader who feels its weight is not to be dismissed, and the volume agrees with Stott that the question must be examined and not ruled out of order."),
("p", "The reply. The volume does not meet the objection with a rule of proportion that the Scripture does not state. The argument that sin against an infinite God deserves infinite punishment is used by Woodier and by older traditionalists, and the volume does not use it, because no verse states it. It answers from what Scripture does say. The Judge of all the earth does right (Genesis 18:25). His judgments are “true and righteous” (Revelation 16:7; 19:2), and Paul’s answer to the question whether God is unrighteous who taketh vengeance is, “God forbid: for then how shall God judge the world?” (Romans 3:5, 6). The judgment is graded (chapter 8). The judgment is of persons by their works, and it is the Lord’s own judgment, spoken by the Lord Himself in the passages the volume has read. Saville, who rejects annihilationism, argues in his article that “extinction cannot be an infinite punishment”, because a punishment that is retributive must be experienced, and he concludes against several traditionalists that extinction is a punishment. The volume notes that his argument is a doctrinal one and does not use it as exegesis. It notes also that the justice objection, taken as an exegetical argument, is an argument from what the reader thinks God would do, and that the volume’s task is to say what the text says He will do."),
("sub", "9.7 The objection from the immortality of the soul"),
("p", "The objection. The idea that the soul is by nature immortal came into the church from Greek philosophy, and it distorted the reading of the passages on the end of the lost. Saville names Travis, Pinnock, Froom and White among those who made the point. Once the idea is set aside, the passages on destruction say what they appear to say."),
("p", "What is granted. The doctrine of the soul’s natural immortality is not stated in Scripture in so many words, and the volume does not rest on it. Matthew 10:28 says that God is able to destroy the soul."),
("p", "The reply. The volume’s case does not depend on the soul’s immortality, and Saville’s own judgment of the argument is that it is not decisive. A person may hold that only God has immortality in the sense of life in himself, and that the lost are kept in being by God’s will, and still hold that the passages on the lake describe a continuing experience. The persons are in the lake because the text puts them there and says they are tormented. That is a statement about what God does with them, and it does not require a doctrine of what they are."),
("sub", "9.8 The objection from the earthly judgments of the wicked"),
("p", "The objection. Many passages say that the wicked shall be no more. “For yet a little while, and the wicked shall not be: yea, thou shalt diligently consider his place, and it shall not be” (Psalm 37:10). “The wicked shall perish… into smoke shall they consume away” (Psalm 37:20). The day that cometh “shall burn as an oven; and all the proud, yea, and all that do wickedly, shall be stubble”, and “they shall be ashes under the soles of your feet” (Malachi 4:1, 3). “The soul that sinneth, it shall die” (Ezekiel 18:4). And at the end, fire from God out of heaven “devoured them” (Revelation 20:9)."),
("p", "What is granted. These verses say that the wicked do not last. They are among the strongest in the conditionalist case, and they are true statements of what happens to the wicked in the settings they describe."),
("p", "The reply. The settings are earthly. Psalm 37 is about who shall inherit the land: “the meek shall inherit the earth” (Psalm 37:11), and the wicked “shall not be” in the place that the righteous dwell in. The prophecy of Malachi is of the day of the Lord on Israel’s land, in which the wicked are stubble, and the verse does not say what comes after. Ezekiel 18 answers the proverb of the fathers and the children in the land of Israel (Ezekiel 18:2, 3) and speaks of the death and life of Israelites under the law in their own day. The fire of Revelation 20:9 devours a living host that has surrounded the camp of the saints, and the next verse, with the devil cast into the lake, speaks of what follows. In all five the verb is of what happens to the living in the age in which they live. The volume grants that these passages speak of the end of the wicked on the earth and does not say that they speak of the lake. It says that they cannot be set against the passages that do."),
("ctbl", "The objections, what is granted and the reply.", [
    ("Objection", "Held by", "What is granted", "The reply"),
    ("The words of destruction mean an ending", "Fudge; Atkinson (as Peterson reports)", "They do in many verses", "They also name a loss of a living person; the passage decides (chapters 3 to 5)"),
    ("Eternal names a result", "Atkinson (as Peterson reports); Fudge", "It does in Hebrews 9:12; 5:9; 6:2", "The persons go into the punishment as into life; the adjective is not carrying the case alone (chapter 6)"),
    ("The fire and worm consume", "Atkinson (as Peterson reports); Fudge", "Isaiah 66:24 is a picture of corpses", "The Lord casts living persons into the place (chapter 7)"),
    ("The smoke is a monument", "Atkinson (as Peterson reports)", "Isaiah 34 and Revelation 19:3 use smoke so", "No rest day nor night and the plural statement of torment are not answered (chapter 8)"),
    ("Endless punishment is unjust", "Stott; Pinnock; Wenham", "The question is earnest", "No verse states the proportion; the Judge does right and the punishment is graded"),
    ("The soul’s immortality is Greek", "Pinnock; others, per Saville", "The doctrine is not stated in Scripture", "The case does not depend on it"),
    ("The wicked shall be no more", "Fudge; Atkinson (as Peterson reports)", "The verses say so", "They speak of the earth and of the living; they are not set against the lake"),
  ], [0.25, 0.17, 0.25, 0.33]),
("sub", "9.9 What the objections leave"),
("p", "The writers on the other side have asked real questions and the volume has tried to answer them without making their case weaker than it is. The reader who has followed the replies will see where they come to rest. The words of destruction do not decide the question in either direction. The adjective of duration can be read as a result and also as a state. The picture of the fire and the worm is a picture of corpses in Isaiah and of a casting in the Gospels. The smoke may be a monument. What remains, when all of this is granted, is the statement of Revelation about persons who are tormented, who have no rest day nor night, and whose plural statement of torment at Revelation 20:10 takes in the beast and the false prophet cast in a thousand years before. The volume holds that this remains because it is in the text, and that nothing in the objections has removed it."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Five writers who hold that the lost cease to exist are named, and three who answer them, with a survey of the debate; modern writers are quoted only where their words were read in a free full text.",
    "The objection from the words of destruction is granted in part: the words describe an ending in many verses; they also describe the loss of persons who live.",
    "The objection from the word eternal is granted in part: the adjective can name a result; the persons go into the punishment as into life.",
    "The objections from the fire and the worm and from the smoke are granted at the points where the Old Testament uses the images of a finished ruin, and answered from the Lord’s use of the picture and from the sentences about persons in Revelation.",
    "The objection from justice is answered from what Scripture says of the Judge and of the grading of judgment, and not from a rule of proportion that Scripture does not state.",
    "The objection from the earthly judgments of the wicked is answered by the settings of the passages, which speak of the living on the earth.",
  ]),
]
